{{also|facade}}
==English==

===Alternative spellings===
* [[facade]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr}} {{term|façade|lang=fr}}, from {{etyl|it}} {{term|facciata}}, a derivation of {{term|faccia||front}}, from {{etyl|la}} {{term|facies||face}}; compare {{term|face}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/fəˈsɑːd/}}<ref name="COED-pron">The '''Concise Oxford English Dictionary''' [Eleventh Edition]</ref>

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{architecture}} The face of a building, especially the front view or [[elevation]].
#* '''1865''', [[w:James Fergusson|James Fergusson]], ''A History of Architecture in All Countries'':
#*: In Egypt the '''façades''' of their rock-cut tombs were ... ornamented so simply and unobtrusively as rather to belie than to announce their internal magnificence.
#* '''1880''', [[w:Charles Eliot Norton|Charles Eliot Norton]], ''Historical Studies of Church-Building in the Middle Ages'':
#*: Like so many of the finest churches, [the cathedral of Siena] was furnished with a plain substantial front wall, intended to serve as the backing and support of an ornamental '''façade'''.
# {{figuratively}} A [[deceptive]] outward appearance.
# {{rfv-sense}} {{context|Organ building}} the [[prospect]]

====Quotations====
* '''1812''': Antonio de Alcedo and George Alexander Thompson [tr.], ''The geographical and historical dictionary of America and the West Indies: containing an entire translation of the Spanish work of Colonel Don Antonio de Alcedo … with large additions and compilations from modern voyages and travels, and from original and authentic information'', volume 2, [http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=yGQFAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA13&dq=%22are+regularly+laid+out+in+lots+along+the+sea+shore,+called+fa%C3%A7ades%22&ei=vNzYSY3ADYWoNurZ2PsC page 13], “Demerara” (J. Carpenter)
*: The plantations are regularly laid out in lots along the sea-shore, called '''''façades''''', about a quarter of a mile wide, and extending ¼ths of a mile back into the country.

====Usage notes====
{{unreferenced}}
* The forms '''facade''' and '''façade''' are alternative spellings of the same word.  The spelling with the [[cedilla]] is preferred by some writers, to reflect the word’s [[French]] origin, to emphasize the [[sibilant]] in the pronunciation, or just because it’s the spelling they believe is correct or it’s the preferred spelling in their dictionary.  Some readers, however, will find any word spelled with accents or diacritics to be distracting or affected.

